---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.mjs';
---

<Base title="About" description="What this project is, what it is for, and how it is built in public.">
  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This is a cycle-accurate 6502 and 65C02 core in C#, and the aim of implementing as many 6502-family machines as
          possible on it, each one proven. It is built in public: the design, the plan, the code and a journal of every
          step are in <a href={SITE.repo}>the repository</a>.
        </p>
        <h2>What proven means</h2>
        <p>
          The core is checked against Tom Harte's SingleStepTests, a published record of every opcode's bus activity, cycle
          by cycle, against whole programs that check their own results, and against a model of the chip's transistors. A machine counts as implemented only when it runs in
          the browser and passes an automated test in CI. Where the work knowingly differs from a reference, that is
          written down on the <a href="/status/">status page</a>.
        </p>
        <h2>Nothing typed by hand</h2>
        <p>
          The figures on the home and status pages are read from the test results of the latest build and from dated
          measurements. The build fails if a figure is missing.
        </p>
        <h2>Illustrations</h2>
        <p>
          The illustrations here were generated. They are captioned as such, and none is used as evidence. The
          die is a plausible one drawn by a model, not the 6502's real layout.
        </p>
        <h2>Licence</h2>
        <p>MIT, apart from what is somebody else's. A running machine's system ROMs are committed to the repository with where each copy came from and what is known of its rights, checked against their hashes when the site is built, and the machine's page says whose they are. The photographs of the original machines keep the licences their sources give them, named under each, and where a source states no licence the credit says so. No commercial game and no third-party test program is committed: the games that are, the BBC Micro's preset discs and the NES's bundled game, are free software and homebrew whose authors let them be shared, each with its licence recorded beside it, and any other game is the visitor's own, read in their browser and never uploaded.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
